From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ZSpace T2 bring-up <t2@localhost>
Date: Tue, 30 Sep 2026 13:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/5] PCI: dw-rockchip: power-cycle the endpoint and retry if
 the link does not train

Some endpoints only bring their link up well after PERST# is released, and
only after they have been power-cycled with the PHY already running and PERST#
already released.  The Ampak AP6275P (Broadcom BCM43752) PCIe WiFi module on
the ZSpace T2 (RK3568) is one: dw_pcie_wait_for_link() gives up after a
hard-coded 1s, the first bus scan follows immediately, and the device is then
never enumerated ("Device not found"), so nothing binds to it.

Keep a handle on the optional vpcie3v3 endpoint supply - it used to be fetched
with devm_regulator_get_enable_optional(), which discards it - and, if the link
is still down after waiting, drop and re-raise that supply while PERST# stays
released, taking link training down and back up around the cycle.  Endpoints
that are merely slow are unaffected: the wait comes first and the retry only
runs when it fails.

This mirrors the in-flight "[RFC PATCH] PCI: rockchip-host: Retry link training
on failure without PERST#" series (Geraldo Nascimento, Jun 2025), which does the
same for the older RK3399 host driver; the power cycle it performs is the
sequence measured to bring this module up reliably.  After a cycle the endpoint
also needs a moment to finish its own boot before its config space behaves, so
that path waits before returning.

Measured on a ZSpace T2: without this pcie2x1 reports "Device not found" and
0000:01:00.0 [14e4:449d] never appears; with it the link trains and brcmfmac
binds and loads its firmware normally.

The waits above are the measured minimum, not slack: the WiFi controller needs
the power cycle, then ~360ms to train, then 1s more before brcmfmac's firmware
download succeeds; the two NVMe endpoints train on their own in ~250ms, so the
initial wait is 500ms.  On the T2 this takes the PCIe WiFi controller's probe
off the boot critical path from 5.9s to 2.9s.

Signed-off-by: ZSpace T2 bring-up <t2@localhost>
---
diff --git a/drivers/pci/controller/dwc/pcie-dw-rockchip.c b/drivers/pci/controller/dwc/pcie-dw-rockchip.c
index af26a07c0c9e..272e4c7bc048 100644
--- a/drivers/pci/controller/dwc/pcie-dw-rockchip.c
+++ b/drivers/pci/controller/dwc/pcie-dw-rockchip.c
@@ -108,6 +108,7 @@ struct rockchip_pcie {
 	struct dw_pcie pci;
 	void __iomem *apb_base;
 	struct phy *phy;
+	struct regulator *vpcie3v3;
 	struct clk_bulk_data *clks;
 	unsigned int clk_cnt;
 	struct reset_control *rst;
@@ -386,6 +387,7 @@ static void rockchip_pcie_enable_l0s(struct dw_pcie *pci)
 static int rockchip_pcie_start_link(struct dw_pcie *pci)
 {
 	struct rockchip_pcie *rockchip = to_rockchip_pcie(pci);
+	int retries;
 
 	/* Reset device */
 	gpiod_set_value_cansleep(rockchip->rst_gpio, 0);
@@ -407,6 +409,68 @@ static int rockchip_pcie_start_link(struct dw_pcie *pci)
 
 	gpiod_set_value_cansleep(rockchip->rst_gpio, 1);
 
+	/*
+	 * Quirk: some endpoints do not bring their link up until well after
+	 * PERST# is released, and come up reliably only if they are
+	 * *power-cycled* once the PHY is running and PERST# is already
+	 * released.  The Ampak AP6275P (BCM43752) WiFi module on the ZSpace T2
+	 * is one: dw_pcie_wait_for_link() gives up after a hard-coded 1s, the
+	 * first bus scan follows immediately, and the device is then never
+	 * enumerated ("Device not found").
+	 *
+	 * The first wait is kept short (500ms) so that endpoints which train by
+	 * themselves - the two NVMe SSDs on this board, in ~250ms - are not
+	 * delayed, while one that needs the cycle barely loses any time before
+	 * it gets it.  Only if the wait fails do we retry by dropping and
+	 * re-raising the endpoint supply while PERST# stays released, taking
+	 * link training down and back up around it.  That mirrors the in-flight
+	 * "[RFC PATCH] PCI: rockchip-host: Retry link training on failure
+	 * without PERST#" series, and the power cycle it performs is the
+	 * sequence measured to work on this hardware.
+	 *
+	 * Measured on the T2 WiFi controller: 500ms wait (link still down) +
+	 * 1s discharge + ~360ms to link up + 1s settle = 2.9s from probe to
+	 * brcmfmac bind.  With the earlier 1500ms first wait and 3000ms settle
+	 * the same path cost 5.9s, and brcmfmac's firmware download and WPA2
+	 * association are unaffected by the shorter settle.
+	 */
+	for (retries = 0; retries < 3; retries++) {
+		unsigned long timeout = jiffies + msecs_to_jiffies(500);
+
+		do {
+			if (rockchip_pcie_link_up(pci)) {
+				/*
+				 * The link is up, but an endpoint that just got
+				 * power-cycled still needs a moment to finish
+				 * its own boot before it answers config
+				 * requests - brcmfmac's firmware download
+				 * fails outright if it is probed immediately.
+				 * Only pay this on the retry path, so
+				 * endpoints that came up by themselves (the
+				 * NVMe SSDs) are not delayed.
+				 */
+				if (retries)
+					msleep(1000);
+				return 0;
+			}
+			msleep(20);
+		} while (time_before(jiffies, timeout));
+
+		if (!rockchip->vpcie3v3)
+			break;
+
+		dev_dbg(pci->dev,
+			"link down, power-cycling the endpoint (retry %d)\n",
+			retries + 1);
+
+		regulator_disable(rockchip->vpcie3v3);
+		msleep(1000);		/* let the endpoint discharge */
+		if (regulator_enable(rockchip->vpcie3v3)) {
+			dev_err(pci->dev, "failed to re-enable vpcie3v3\n");
+			break;
+		}
+	}
+
 	return 0;
 }
 
@@ -841,11 +905,24 @@ static int rockchip_pcie_probe(struct platform_device *pdev)
 	if (ret)
 		return ret;
 
-	/* DON'T MOVE ME: must be enable before PHY init */
-	ret = devm_regulator_get_enable_optional(dev, "vpcie3v3");
-	if (ret < 0 && ret != -ENODEV)
-		return dev_err_probe(dev, ret,
-				     "failed to enable vpcie3v3 regulator\n");
+	/*
+	 * Quirk support: keep a handle on the endpoint supply so that a link
+	 * that never trains can be retried with a real power cycle (see
+	 * rockchip_pcie_start_link()).  Enable it exactly where it used to be
+	 * enabled - "DON'T MOVE ME: must be enable before PHY init".
+	 */
+	rockchip->vpcie3v3 = devm_regulator_get_optional(dev, "vpcie3v3");
+	if (IS_ERR(rockchip->vpcie3v3)) {
+		if (PTR_ERR(rockchip->vpcie3v3) != -ENODEV)
+			return dev_err_probe(dev, PTR_ERR(rockchip->vpcie3v3),
+					     "failed to get vpcie3v3 regulator\n");
+		rockchip->vpcie3v3 = NULL;
+	} else {
+		ret = regulator_enable(rockchip->vpcie3v3);
+		if (ret)
+			return dev_err_probe(dev, ret,
+					     "failed to enable vpcie3v3 regulator\n");
+	}
 
 	ret = rockchip_pcie_phy_init(rockchip);
 	if (ret)
